        }
      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "Skill Resolution Flow (Trace 2)\n├── findSkill() entry \u003c-- cmd/foxctl/cmd/skill_helpers.go:87\n│   ├── createSkillResolver(cfg) \u003c-- 2a\n│   │   └── builds search paths from:\n│   │       ├── EnvSearchPaths() \u003c-- internal/domain/skill/searchpaths.go:10\n│   │       ├── UserSearchPaths() \u003c-- internal/domain/skill/searchpaths.go:18\n│   │       ├── BuiltinSearchPaths() \u003c-- internal/domain/skill/searchpaths.go:26\n│   │       └── DevSearchPaths() \u003c-- internal/domain/skill/searchpaths.go:34\n│   ├── resolver.Resolve(requested) \u003c-- 2b\n│   │   └── resolveFromSearchPaths() \u003c-- 2c\n│   │       └── for each basePath: \u003c-- internal/domain/skill/resolver.go:175\n│   │           └── filepath.Join() \u003c-- 2d\n│   │               └── check skill.yaml exists \u003c-- internal/domain/skill/resolver.go:182\n│   └── loadSkillDir(dir) \u003c-- cmd/foxctl/cmd/skill_helpers.go:128\n│       ├── LoadManifest(manifestPath) \u003c-- 2e\n│       │   └── yaml.Unmarshal + validate \u003c-- internal/domain/skill/manifest.go:117\n│       └── ResolveArtifactPath() \u003c-- 2f\n│           └── finds bin/bin-cgo/module.wasm \u003c-- internal/domain/skill/artifact.go:44\n└── returns SkillHandle{Manifest, Paths} \u003c-- cmd/foxctl/cmd/skill_helpers.go:149",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nSkills in foxctl are **self-contained executables** (native binaries or WebAssembly modules) that live in directories with a `skill.yaml` manifest. Users invoke them by name like `foxctl run text/grep`, but the system needs to **find the skill directory** by searching multiple filesystem locations. The resolver implements this discovery mechanism, checking environment paths, user directories (`~/.foxctl/skills`), builtin locations (next to the executable), and development paths in priority order [2a].\n\n## Details\n\n### Search Path Construction\n\nThe resolver builds a prioritized list of directories to search [2a]:\n1. **`FOXCTL_SKILLS_PATH`** environment variable (highest priority)\n2. **User directory** at `~/.foxctl/skills`\n3. **Builtin directory** relative to the foxctl executable\n4. **Development paths** like `./dist/skills` and `./skills`\n\nThis ordering allows users to override builtin skills and supports local development workflows.\n\n### Resolution Algorithm\n\nWhen resolving a skill name like `text/grep` [2b]:\n\n1. **Iterate through search paths** in priority order [2c]\n2. **Construct candidate path** by joining the base path with the skill name [2d]\n3. **Check for `skill.yaml`** in that directory - first match wins [2d]\n4. **Load and parse the manifest** from YAML [2e]\n5. **Resolve the artifact path** - finds `bin`, `bin-cgo`, or `module.wasm` depending on the distribution type [2f]\n\nThe system returns a **`SkillHandle`** containing the parsed manifest, manifest path, and artifact path - everything needed to execute the skill.\n\n### Name Normalization\n\nThe resolver tries both the original name and a normalized version (replacing `/` and `-` with `_`) to handle different naming conventions across platforms."
    },
    {
      "id": "3",
      "title": "Input Loading from Multiple Sources",
      "description": "Input handling layer supporting inline JSON, files, stdin envelopes, and CAS digests",
      "locations": [
        {
          "id": "3a",
          "path": "cmd/foxctl/cmd/skill_helpers.go",
          "lineNumber": 26,
          "lineContent": "func loadSkillInput(cmd *cobra.Command, cfg config.Config, inline, file string) ([]byte, error) {",
          "title": "Input loading entry point",
          "description": "Handles multiple input sources based on flags"
        },
        {
          "id": "3b",
          "path": "cmd/foxctl/cmd/skill_helpers.go",
          "lineNumber": 31,
          "lineContent": "case file != \"\":\n\t\treturn os.ReadFile(file)",
          "title": "Load from file path",
          "description": "Reads input JSON from specified file"
        },
        {
          "id": "3c",
          "path": "cmd/foxctl/cmd/skill_helpers.go",
          "lineNumber": 33,
          "lineContent": "case strings.EqualFold(trimmed, \"stdin\"):\n\t\tdata, err := extractEnvelopeData(cmd.InOrStdin())",
          "title": "Extract data from stdin envelope",
          "description": "Parses envelope JSON and extracts data field"
        },
        {
          "id": "3d",
          "path": "cmd/foxctl/cmd/skill_helpers.go",
          "lineNumber": 39,
          "lineContent": "case strings.HasPrefix(trimmed, \"sha256:\"):",
          "title": "Load from CAS digest",
          "description": "Retrieves content from content-addressable storage"
        },
        {
          "id": "3e",
          "path": "cmd/foxctl/cmd/skill_helpers.go",
          "lineNumber": 49,
          "lineContent": "rc, _, err := store.Get(ctx, trimmed)",
          "title": "Fetch from CAS store",
          "description": "Opens reader for CAS object by digest"
        },
        {
          "id": "3f",
          "path": "cmd/foxctl/cmd/skill_helpers.go",
          "lineNumber": 62,
          "lineContent": "case trimmed != \"\":\n\t\treturn []byte(inline), nil",
          "title": "Use inline JSON",
          "description": "Returns inline flag value as input bytes"
        }
      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "Input Loading Flow (Trace 3)\n└── loadSkillInput() entry \u003c-- 3a\n    ├── Branch: file path provided? \u003c-- cmd/foxctl/cmd/skill_helpers.go:31\n    │   └── os.ReadFile(file) \u003c-- 3b\n    ├── Branch: stdin mode? \u003c-- cmd/foxctl/cmd/skill_helpers.go:33\n    │   └── extractEnvelopeData() \u003c-- 3c\n    │       └── json.Unmarshal envelope \u003c-- cmd/foxctl/cmd/skill_helpers.go:74\n    │           └── extract data field \u003c-- cmd/foxctl/cmd/skill_helpers.go:84\n    ├── Branch: CAS digest (sha256:)? \u003c-- 3d\n    │   ├── cas.NewStore() open \u003c-- cmd/foxctl/cmd/skill_helpers.go:40\n    │   └── store.Get(digest) \u003c-- 3e\n    │       └── io.ReadAll from CAS \u003c-- cmd/foxctl/cmd/skill_helpers.go:53\n    ├── Branch: inline JSON string? \u003c-- cmd/foxctl/cmd/skill_helpers.go:62\n    │   └── return []byte(inline) \u003c-- 3f\n    └── Default: empty input \u003c-- cmd/foxctl/cmd/skill_helpers.go:64\n        └── return []byte(\"{}\") \u003c-- cmd/foxctl/cmd/skill_helpers.go:65",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nSkills in foxctl can receive input data in multiple ways: as inline JSON strings, from files, from stdin (when piped from another command), or from content-addressable storage (CAS) using digest references. The **input loading system** [3a] provides a unified interface that handles all these sources transparently, allowing users to choose the most convenient method for their workflow without changing how skills are invoked.\n\nThis flexibility is critical because:\n- **Inline JSON** works for simple, short inputs during development\n- **Files** are needed for complex or reusable input payloads\n- **Stdin mode** enables composable pipelines where one skill's output becomes another's input\n- **CAS digests** allow referencing large inputs without passing them inline, reducing overhead\n\n## Details\n\nThe loading logic is implemented as a **switch statement** that branches based on input source flags [3a]. Each branch handles a specific source:\n\n**File loading** [3b] is straightforward—when `--input-file` is provided, the system reads the file path directly using `os.ReadFile`. The special value `\"-\"` reads from stdin as raw bytes.\n\n**Stdin envelope mode** [3c] is more sophisticated. When the input flag equals `\"stdin\"`, the system expects a complete envelope JSON on stdin and extracts only the `data` field. This enables chaining: `foxctl run skill1 | foxctl run skill2 --input stdin` passes skill1's output data as skill2's input.\n\n**CAS digest loading** [3d, 3e] handles inputs prefixed with `sha256:`. The system opens the CAS store, retrieves the object by digest, and reads its contents. This is useful for large inputs that have been previously stored, avoiding repeated transmission of bulky data.\n\n**Inline JSON** [3f] is the default path—any non-empty string that doesn't match the special cases above is treated as literal JSON input bytes.\n\n**Empty default** returns `{}` when no input is specified, providing a valid empty JSON object that skills can safely parse.\n\nAll paths converge to return `[]byte`, providing a consistent interface regardless of source. Error handling is specific to each source type (file not found, invalid CAS digest, malformed envelope JSON, etc.)."
    },
    {
      "id": "4",
      "title": "Ephemeral Execution (Fast Path)",
      "description": "Runservice ephemeral mode that skips job persistence for ~60% latency improvement in hook scenarios",
      "locations": [
        {
          "id": "4a",
          "path": "internal/runservice/ephemeral.go",
          "lineNumber": 24,
          "lineContent": "func (e *Executor) ExecuteEphemeral(input []byte) error {",
          "title": "Ephemeral execution entry",
          "description": "Fast path without job store or trajectory capture"
        },
        {
          "id": "4b",
          "path": "internal/runservice/ephemeral.go",
          "lineNumber": 33,
          "lineContent": "skillExecutor := execution.NewRunnerExecutor()",
          "title": "Create skill executor",
          "description": "Instantiates runner adapter for skill execution"
        },
        {
          "id": "4c",
          "path": "internal/runservice/ephemeral.go",
          "lineNumber": 59,
          "lineContent": "result, err := skillExecutor.Execute(ctx, execution.ExecuteOptions{",
          "title": "Execute skill directly",
          "description": "Runs skill without job infrastructure overhead"
        },
        {
          "id": "4d",
          "path": "internal/runservice/ephemeral.go",
          "lineNumber": 77,
          "lineContent": "return e.handleEphemeralSuccess(result.Stdout)",
          "title": "Handle success result",
          "description": "Validates envelope and annotates with ephemeral metadata"
        },
        {
          "id": "4e",
          "path": "internal/runservice/ephemeral.go",
          "lineNumber": 132,
          "lineContent": "annotated := annotateCorrelationAndJob(stdout, \"\", e.options.CorrelationID)",
          "title": "Annotate with correlation ID",
          "description": "Adds correlation metadata to envelope without job ID"
        },
        {
          "id": "4f",
          "path": "internal/runservice/ephemeral.go",
          "lineNumber": 136,
          "lineContent": "if err := writeEnvelope(e.stdout, annotated); err != nil {",
          "title": "Write result to stdout",
          "description": "Outputs annotated envelope to CLI stdout"
        }
      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "Ephemeral Execution Flow (Fast Path)\n├── ExecuteEphemeral() entry \u003c-- 4a\n│   ├── TryServeCache() (cache read-only check) \u003c-- internal/runservice/ephemeral.go:26\n│   ├── NewRunnerExecutor() instantiation \u003c-- 4b\n│   ├── skillExecutor.Execute() call \u003c-- 4c\n│   │   └── RunnerExecutor.Execute() \u003c-- internal/execution/runner_adapter.go:21\n│   │       └── runner.RunWithOptions() \u003c-- internal/execution/runner_adapter.go:28\n│   │           └── [exec/wasi runner execution]\n│   ├── handleEphemeralSuccess() \u003c-- 4d\n│   │   ├── json.Unmarshal() validate envelope \u003c-- internal/runservice/ephemeral.go:124\n│   │   ├── annotateCorrelationAndJob() \u003c-- 4e\n│   │   └── writeEnvelope() to stdout \u003c-- 4f\n│   └── (skips: job store, trajectory, cache write)",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nWhen running skills via hooks (like git pre-commit hooks), the standard execution path with full job persistence adds **~150ms of overhead** from database operations, state tracking, and trajectory capture. For hooks that need to run quickly and don't require historical job records, this overhead is wasteful. **Ephemeral execution mode** provides a **~60% latency improvement** by skipping all persistence infrastructure while still executing the skill and returning results [4a].\n\nThe key tradeoff: ephemeral mode sacrifices job history and observability for speed. It's designed for transient operations where you care about the immediate result but not about tracking the execution in the job database.\n\n## Details\n\n### Entry Point\n\nEphemeral execution begins when the `--ephemeral` flag is set, causing the executor to call `ExecuteEphemeral()` instead of the standard sync path [4a]. This bypasses job store initialization entirely.\n\n### Execution Flow\n\nThe flow is deliberately minimal:\n\n1. **Cache check** (read-only): Still attempts to serve from cache for deduplication, but skips cache writes to avoid persistence overhead\n2. **Direct execution**: Creates a `RunnerExecutor` [4b] and immediately calls `Execute()` [4c] without any job infrastructure\n3. **Result validation**: Unmarshals the stdout to validate it's a proper envelope structure [4d]\n4. **Metadata annotation**: Adds correlation ID to the envelope meta without a job ID (since no job was created) [4e]\n5. **Output**: Writes the annotated envelope directly to stdout [4f]\n\n### What Gets Skipped\n\nEphemeral mode intentionally avoids:\n- **Job store operations**: No database open, no job record creation, no state transitions (~30-50ms saved)\n- **Trajectory capture**: No execution trace recording for observability (~20ms saved)\n- **Cache writes**: Results aren't persisted to cache (~30ms saved)\n- **Job directory creation**: No filesystem artifacts created\n\n### Environment Propagation\n\nEven in ephemeral mode, the execution still propagates trace context and workspace information to the skill process via environment variables. The `ExtraEnv` parameter allows passing variables like `FOXCTL_NO_CAS=1` directly to the child process without race conditions from `os.Setenv`.\n\n### Error Handling\n\nErrors are still wrapped in proper envelope format. If the skill returns an error envelope (status=\"error\"), ephemeral mode outputs it directly. If execution fails at the process level, it constructs an error envelope from stderr and the error message."
    },
